#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125769 :tutorial/content "To multiply square roots, we simply multiply the numbers under the root. This is called the **product rule for radicals**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}$ by applying the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\nThe product rule also works for cube roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5} = \\sqrt[3]{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[3]{10}.\n$$\n\nIn general, the product rule works for any product of $n$th roots. So for any $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{2} \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{5} = \\sqrt[n]{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[n]{10}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot use the product rule when we have the product of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression $\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt[3]{5}$ cannot be simplified because the radicals $\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$ are different." :tutorial/id #uuid "f7f60ab3-2c97-5570-bacc-49ef3d371c52" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3296 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125770 :tutorial/content "The product rule can often be used to simplify square roots.\n\nFor example, consider $\\sqrt{98}.$ Note that $98=49 \\cdot 2,$ where $49$ is a perfect square. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{98} = \\sqrt{49 \\cdot 2} = \\sqrt{49} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = 7 \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nAlternatively, if we're given a simplified square root and we want to convert it to the square root of a single number, then we can work backwards using the product rule:\n\n$$\n7 \\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{49} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{49 \\cdot 2} = \\sqrt{98}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6b8006c0-4a18-5c9d-84c6-1224cf85a921" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3298 :tutorial/title "Simplifying Square Roots"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125771 :tutorial/content "The product rule applies to cube roots as well.\n\nFor example, consider $\\sqrt[3]{24}.$ Note that $24=8 \\cdot 3,$ where $8$ is a perfect cube. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{24} = \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = 2 \\sqrt[3]{3}.\n$$\n\nAgain, if we're given a simplified cube root and we want to convert it to the cube root of a single number then we can work backwards using the product rule:\n\n$$\n2 \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{24}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d8fc754e-e350-5273-9188-9203a384b9b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3299 :tutorial/title "The Product Rule With Cube Roots"}]}
